const PRE=[{\"id\":\"rotisserie_chicken\",\"title\":\"Rotisserie chicken from the hot case\",\"aliases\":[\"rotisserie chicken\",\"rotisserie\",\"deli chicken\",\"hot chicken\",\"cooked whole chicken\",\"store roasted chicken\",\"seasoned rotisserie chicken\",\"is rotisserie chicken healthy\",\"what is in a rotisserie chicken\",\"is the deli rotisserie chicken injected\"],\"category\":\"poultry\",\"question\":\"Is the rotisserie chicken worth buying?\",\"decision\":\"Chicken and salt on the label: buy. Phosphate and starch: pass.\",\"why\":\"Every ingredient must be listed in descending order; a solution names its percent and contents.\",\"short_answer\":\"A rotisserie bird is a plain chicken or a solution bird. A plain one is chicken with salt and spices, roasted in the store. A solution bird was pumped with a seasoning solution before the spit. The sticker says which.\",\"detail\":\"Two label rules do the work. 9 CFR 381.117(h): a raw poultry product with added solution must carry a descriptive designation stating the percentage of added solution as a number with the % sign, e.g. ‘contains 15% added solution of water and salt’, plus the common or usual name of every solution ingredient in descending order; the word ‘enhanced’ may not appear in the product name. 9 CFR 381.118(a): any poultry product fabricated from two or more ingredients lists them by common or usual name in descending order of predominance; ingredients at 2 percent or less may trail the list under a ‘contains 2% or less of’ line. 9 CFR 424.21 permits sodium and potassium phosphates in poultry ‘to decrease the amount of cooked out juices’, capped at 0.5 percent of total product, and sodium hydroxide only alongside phosphate at one part to four. FSIS’s labeling glossary defines a bone-in bird injected or marinated with broth, water, fat or flavor enhancers as ‘basted’ or ‘self basted’, about 3 percent solution, with the solution ingredients named on the label. At the shelf the two kinds read differently: a plain bird’s panel is one line (Whole Foods Market’s organic rotisserie chicken lists ‘organic chicken’ and nothing else); a solution bird lists water and its seasoning after the chicken, in descending order as 381.118 requires; a phosphate, a starch and a sweetener are the usual entries. On the sticker, the second word on the list settles it: ‘salt’ or ‘spices’ is a plain bird; ‘water’ followed by ‘sodium phosphate’, ‘modified food starch’, ‘sugar’ is a solution bird, roasted. Both are cooked chicken. One of them is also a marinade injection.\",\"evidence_tier\":\"kristys_standard\",\"sources\":[\"9 CFR § 381.117 Name of product and other labeling (law.cornell.edu) https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/9/381.117\",\"9 CFR § 381.118 Ingredients statement (law.cornell.edu) https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/9/381.118\",\"9 CFR § 424.21 Use of food ingredients and sources of radiation (law.cornell.edu) https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/9/424.21\",\"USDA FSIS, Chicken from Farm to Table, Ready-Prepared Chicken https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/poultry/chicken-farm-table\",\"USDA FSIS, ‘Danger Zone’ (40°F - 140°F) https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/danger-zone-40f-140f\",\"USDA FSIS, Meat and Poultry Labeling Terms, Basted / Self basted https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/meat-and-poultry-labeling-terms\",\"Whole Foods Market Organic Rotisserie Chicken, ingredient list from the package (EWG Food Scores, UPC 099482803568) https://www.ewg.org/foodscores/products/099482803568-WholeFoodsMarketOrganicRotisserieChicken/\"],\"kristy_take\":\"A roasted chicken should list a chicken. When the list runs to phosphate and starch, the seasoning went in with a needle.\",\"instead\":\"A plain raw bird from the case, roasted at home. Salt it the night before; the skin does the rest.\",\"cart_pick\":\"Plain rotisserie chicken\",\"buying_tips\":[\"Read the sticker, not the name. ‘Seasoned’, ‘original’ and ‘traditional’ are marketing; the ingredient list is regulated.\",\"The shortest list wins: chicken, salt, spices. Water as the second ingredient means a solution was added.\",\"Sodium phosphate, a starch or a sweetener after the water means a solution bird. The shorter the seasoning line, the plainer the chicken.\",\"A bird held under the lamp is held hot for a reason. Take one that is steaming, not one that has sat.\"],\"watch_out\":[\"FSIS: eat or refrigerate a hot rotisserie bird within 2 hours of purchase. If it will not be eaten in that window, cut it up. Refrigerate it in shallow covered containers.\",\"Hot food is held at or above 140°F. A bird sitting in the case long enough to fall below that has left its hold. FSIS puts the growth range at 40 to 140°F.\",\"Eat within 3 to 4 days, cold or reheated to 165°F. Freezing is fine; use within 4 months for texture.\"],\"pick_steps\":[\"Read the sticker’s ingredient list, not the name: chicken, salt, spices wins.\",\"Water second, then phosphate, starch or sweetener, means a solution bird.\",\"Take one that is steaming under the lamp, not one that has sat.\"],\"science\":\"‘Seasoned’, ‘original’ and ‘traditional’ are marketing; the ingredient list is regulated and runs in descending order. A solution names its contents, so the second word on the list settles it. Hot food is held at or above 140°F. FSIS puts the growth range at 40 to 140°F. Eat or refrigerate a hot rotisserie bird within 2 hours of purchase.\",\"labels_decoded\":[{\"term\":\"Contains up to X% solution\",\"meaning\":\"Required wording under 9 CFR 381.117(h) for a raw bird with added solution: the percent as a number, then every solution ingredient in descending order. ‘Enhanced’ is not allowed in the product name.\"},{\"term\":\"Basted / self basted\",\"meaning\":\"FSIS term for a bone-in bird injected or marinated with broth, water, fat, spices or flavor enhancers, about 3 percent solution, with the ingredients named on the label.\"},{\"term\":\"Sodium phosphate\",\"meaning\":\"A permitted poultry ingredient under 9 CFR 424.21 ‘to decrease the amount of cooked out juices’, capped at 0.5 percent of total product. On a rotisserie sticker it marks a solution bird.\"}],\"tier_note\":\"Two federal label rules settle it; the ingredient list just needs reading.\",\"eyebrow_short\":\"Rotisserie chicken\",\"asked_as\":[\"is rotisserie chicken healthy\",\"what is in a rotisserie chicken\",\"is the deli rotisserie chicken injected\",\"should i buy the rotisserie chicken\",\"does rotisserie chicken have added ingredients\",\"is store roasted chicken okay\"]},{\"id\":\"chicken_cuts_basics\",\"title\":\"Chicken\",\"aliases\":[\"chicken cuts\",\"chicken breast\",\"chicken thigh\",\"chicken thighs\",\"whole chicken\",\"dark meat\",\"white meat\",\"drumstick\",\"which chicken cut\",\"thighs\",\"breasts\",\"thighs or breasts\",\"boneless skinless\",\"duck legs\",\"duck leg\",\"duck meat\",\"air chilled chicken\",\"air-chilled\",\"chicken\",\"water chilled\",\"no antibiotics\",\"raised without antibiotics\",\"antibiotic free chicken\",\"abf\"],\"category\":\"poultry\",\"question\":\"Which cut of chicken should I buy?\",\"decision\":\"Air-chilled first, then bone-in, skin-on thighs over breast.\",\"why\":\"Dark meat is forgiving and hard to overcook. Boneless skinless is the priciest form of it.\",\"short_answer\":\"Whole bird: cheapest per pound, and the carcass makes stock. Thighs carry more fat and flavor, and are hard to overcook. Breast is lean and dries fast. Bone-in, skin-on cuts cost less and cook better than boneless skinless.\",\"detail\":\"Dark meat comes from muscles the bird actually used, so it carries more fat and stays juicy across a wide window of doneness. Breast is a low-use muscle: lean, quick, unforgiving. Bones conduct heat slowly and skin renders and bastes, which is why bone-in skin-on thighs are the most forgiving thing in the case. Boneless skinless breast is convenience paid for twice, once at the register and again in how fast it dries. A whole bird broken down at home gives you both kinds of meat plus a frame for broth, at the lowest cost per pound in the store. Thighs are dark meat: more fat, more flavor, and very hard to overcook. Breast is lean and fast and goes dry the moment you look away. Bone-in and skin-on cost less and cook better than boneless skinless, which is the most expensive way to buy the same bird.\",\"evidence_tier\":\"kristys_standard\",\"sources\":[\"USDA FoodData Central (poultry composition)\",\"USDA FSIS poultry labeling terms\",\"9 CFR § 441.10 “Retained water in raw meat and poultry products” (Cornell LII) https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/9/441.10\",\"USDA FSIS poultry-chilling references\",\"USDA FSIS ‘raised without antibiotics’ guidance\",\"FDA antibiotic-residue rules\",\"Consumer Reports “Decoding the Labels on Meat Packages” https://www.consumerreports.org/food/decoding-the-labels-on-meat-packages/decoding-the-labels-on-meat-packages.html\",\"Rebel Pastures “The Truth About Chicken Labels: What You Need to Know” https://rebelpastures.com/blogs/real-talk-from-the-pasture/the-truth-about-chicken-labels-what-you-need-to-know\"],\"kristy_take\":\"Bone-in thighs are the most forgiving protein in the store. A whole bird is the cheapest, and the frame makes stock.\",\"cart_pick\":\"Air-chilled whole chicken or bone-in thighs\",\"buying_tips\":[\"Whole bird: cheapest per pound, and the carcass makes stock.\",\"Thighs, bone-in and skin-on: forgiving, cheap, hard to overcook.\",\"Breast: fast and lean. Pull it the moment it is done.\",\"Boneless skinless is the priciest form of the same bird.\",\"‘Air-chilled’ is printed on the package when it applies.\",\"Skin that starts dry is the skin that crisps.\",\"‘USDA Process Verified’ or a third-party seal backs a ‘raised without antibiotics’ claim.\",\"All chicken is required to be residue-free at sale, labeled or not.\",\"‘Raised without antibiotics’ is a management and resistance choice, not a cleaner-meat guarantee.\"],\"labels_decoded\":[{\"term\":\"Dark meat (thigh, drumstick)\",\"meaning\":\"Working muscle. More fat, more flavor, a wide margin for error.\"},{\"term\":\"White meat (breast, tenderloin)\",\"meaning\":\"Low-use muscle. Lean and fast, dries out quickly.\"},{\"term\":\"Contains up to X% retained water\",\"meaning\":\"Water absorbed during water-chilling — you’re paying for it by weight.\"},{\"term\":\"Raised without antibiotics\",\"meaning\":\"No antibiotics used in raising — meaningful, best when third-party verified.\"},{\"term\":\"No antibiotic residues\",\"meaning\":\"True of all chicken by law — not a differentiator.\"}],\"tier_note\":\"Water-chilled packages can hold retained water, and thighs carry more fat than breast.\",\"asked_as\":[\"which chicken cut should i buy\",\"thighs or breasts\",\"is boneless skinless worth it\",\"how do you buy duck legs\",\"what is air chilled chicken\",\"is air chilled chicken better\",\"which chicken should i buy\",\"does no antibiotics mean anything\",\"is antibiotic free chicken better\",\"should i pay for no antibiotics\"],\"watch_out\":[\"Boneless skinless is the priciest form of the same bird.\",\"Water-chilled packages disclose ‘retained water up to’ a percentage. That percentage is the tank.\",\"No ‘retained water’ line on the label means none is allowed inside. The disclosure is required, not optional.\",\"Where air-chilled is not stocked, a regular bird is still a clean cheap protein. Pat it dry before it goes in the pan.\",\"Safe handling matters far more than the chilling method.\",\"‘Raised without antibiotics’ rests on the producer’s own affidavit to USDA. The agency does not inspect the farm, so oversight is weak. A third-party seal is what makes it checkable.\",\"‘Natural’ says nothing about antibiotics. It means no artificial ingredients were added after processing and the meat is minimally processed. A natural bird can be raised with antibiotics.\",\"‘No hormones’ on chicken is an empty claim. Hormones and steroids are not allowed in poultry, so every bird already qualifies.\",\"Organic is the seal that carries the antibiotic rule: organic feed only, no antibiotics.\"],\"pick_steps\":[\"Bone-in, skin-on thighs are the forgiving pick; boneless skinless costs the most.\",\"A whole bird is cheapest per pound, and the frame makes stock.\",\"Look for ‘air-chilled’ printed on the package; it appears when it applies.\"],\"science\":\"Dark meat comes from muscles the bird actually used, so it carries more fat and stays juicy. Breast is a low-use muscle: lean, quick and unforgiving, so pull it the moment it is done. Bones conduct heat slowly, and skin renders and bastes.\"},{\"id\":\"pork_cuts_and_enhanced\",\"title\":\"Pork cuts and ‘solution added’ pork\",\"aliases\":[\"pork\",\"pork chops\",\"pork shoulder\",\"pork loin\",\"boston butt\",\"enhanced pork\",\"solution added\",\"brined pork\",\"which pork cut\",\"contains added solution\"],\"category\":\"pork\",\"question\":\"Which pork cut should I buy, and what does ‘solution added’ mean?\",\"decision\":\"Pork whose label carries no added-solution percentage, only pork.\",\"why\":\"Raw meat with added solution must name it, give the percentage and list what is in it.\",\"short_answer\":\"A raw meat product with added solution must say so in its name, with the percentage. One example is ‘contains 15% added solution of water and salt’. The solution’s ingredients are listed in descending order by weight.\",\"detail\":\"A large share of retail pork is pumped with a solution of water, salt and phosphates to hold moisture. The law requires the share as a number with a percent sign, like ‘contains 15% added solution’. It is the solution weighed against the raw meat, so 15% means 15 parts to 100. The ingredients in the solution are listed in descending order by weight. The rule is written for raw meat products, not pork alone.\",\"evidence_tier\":\"established\",\"sources\":[\"USDA FSIS labeling requirements for enhanced and solution-added meat\",\"USDA Institutional Meat Purchase Specifications (pork cut nomenclature)\",\"9 CFR § 317.2 labeling of raw meat and poultry containing added solutions (Cornell LII) https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/9/317.2\"],\"kristy_take\":\"Read the fine print: ‘up to 12% solution’ is water sold by the pound.\",\"cart_pick\":\"Pork shoulder\",\"buying_tips\":[\"Read for ‘contains X% added solution’. That is injected water and salt.\",\"The solution’s ingredients follow, heaviest first.\",\"Hormones are banned in pork production, so ‘no added hormones’ on pork is a freebie.\"],\"labels_decoded\":[{\"term\":\"Enhanced / contains up to X% solution\",\"meaning\":\"Injected with saltwater and phosphates. Declared by law, and sold by weight.\"},{\"term\":\"Natural\",\"meaning\":\"On meat, minimally processed with no artificial ingredients. Says nothing about raising.\"}],\"tier_note\":\"Added solution must be disclosed by weight, which is what makes the fine print worth finding.\",\"asked_as\":[\"which pork cut should i buy\",\"what does solution added mean\",\"what is enhanced pork\",\"what does contains added solution mean\"],\"watch_out\":[\"The word ‘enhanced’ cannot be used in the product name. The law requires a number with a percent sign, like ‘contains 15% added solution’. That figure is what to find.\",\"The percentage is the solution weighed against the raw meat without it. So 15% means 15 parts solution to 100 parts meat.\"],\"science\":\"A large share of retail pork is pumped with water, salt and phosphates to hold moisture. The law requires the share as a number with a percent sign. It is the solution weighed against the raw meat, so 15% means 15 parts to 100. Hormones are banned in pork production, so ‘no added hormones’ is a freebie. ‘Natural’ on meat says nothing about raising.\",\"pick_steps\":[\"Find the ‘% added solution’ figure and the solution’s listed ingredients.\",\"How it was raised: see What “pasture-raised” does and does not tell you.\"]},{\"id\":\"deli_meat_uncured\",\"title\":\"‘Uncured’ deli meat\",\"aliases\":[\"uncured\",\"uncured bacon\",\"uncured hot dogs\",\"deli meat\",\"celery powder\",\"no nitrates added\",\"nitrite free\",\"lunch meat\",\"deli counter\",\"deli turkey\",\"sliced turkey\",\"turkey slices\",\"turkey cold cuts\",\"cold cuts\",\"is deli turkey cured\"],\"category\":\"deli\",\"question\":\"Is ‘uncured’ deli meat actually nitrite-free?\",\"decision\":\"‘Uncured’ meat is cured, using celery powder instead of added nitrite.\",\"why\":\"Celery juice powder is a concentrated natural nitrate source. Same chemistry, different source.\",\"short_answer\":\"No. ‘Uncured’ packages must carry a qualifier, along the lines of ‘except those naturally occurring in celery powder’. Celery juice powder is a concentrated natural nitrate source. So the meat is cured, with a vegetable-derived agent, not a synthetic one.\",\"detail\":\"No, and the package says so if you find the asterisk. ‘Uncured, no nitrates or nitrites added’ is required to be followed by a qualifier along the lines of ‘except those naturally occurring in celery powder’. USDA labeling rules force that qualifier, which is why every ‘uncured’ package carries a footnote most shoppers never read. The chemistry that fixes the color and preserves the meat is the same either way; only the source of the nitrate differs, and measured residual levels are broadly comparable. That does not make it a scam, it makes it a sourcing preference, and it is worth knowing that ‘uncured’ is a different statement from ‘no nitrites’. The bigger question at the deli case is how much processed meat a week holds, not which curing agent was used.\",\"evidence_tier\":\"established\",\"sources\":[\"USDA FSIS labeling policy on ‘uncured’ and ‘no nitrate or nitrite added’ claims\",\"Peer-reviewed comparisons of residual nitrite in conventionally and naturally cured meats\",\"Niche Meat Processor Assistance Network “Uncured Bacon” https://www.nichemeatprocessing.org/uncured-bacon/\",\"9 CFR § 317.17 curing-agent declaration and “uncured” labeling (Cornell LII) https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/9/317.17\"],\"kristy_take\":\"‘Uncured’ means cured with celery powder. The asterisk on the package says so. Same chemistry, different source.\",\"buying_tips\":[\"Find the asterisk. ‘Uncured’ is always qualified on the label.\",\"Celery powder is a natural nitrate source. It is a curing agent, not the absence of one.\",\"Sliced at the counter is usually a shorter ingredient list than the pre-packed tub.\",\"Whole roasted meat sliced thin does the same sandwich job with nothing added.\"],\"labels_decoded\":[{\"term\":\"Uncured\",\"meaning\":\"Cured with a vegetable-derived nitrate source instead of a synthetic one.\"},{\"term\":\"No nitrates or nitrites added*\",\"meaning\":\"The asterisk reads ‘except those naturally occurring in celery powder’.\"}],\"tier_note\":\"Celery powder being the same nitrate chemistry as added nitrite is settled labeling fact, whichever cut the counter slices.\",\"asked_as\":[\"is uncured deli meat nitrite free\",\"what does uncured mean\",\"is deli meat cured with celery\",\"is deli turkey cured\"],\"watch_out\":[\"The qualifier to find reads ‘No nitrate or nitrite added except those naturally occurring in celery juice powder’. That sentence is the whole claim.\",\"Every curing substance has to be named in the ingredients statement, so read the list, not only the asterisk.\"],\"pick_steps\":[\"Find the asterisk: ‘uncured’ is always qualified on the label.\",\"Read the ingredients list; every curing substance has to be named there.\",\"Pick whole roasted meat first, then counter-sliced meat before the prepacked tub.\"],\"science\":\"‘Uncured’ meat is cured, using celery powder instead of added nitrite. Celery powder is a concentrated natural nitrate source, so it is a curing agent, not the absence of one. USDA labeling rules force the qualifier: ‘except those naturally occurring in celery powder’. The chemistry is the same either way; only the source of the nitrate differs. So ‘uncured’ is a different statement from ‘no nitrites’.\"},{\"id\":\"pick_sausage\",\"kind\":\"pick\",\"title\":\"Sausage\",\"category\":\"pork\",\"aliases\":[\"sausage\",\"sausages\",\"italian sausage\",\"italian sausages\",\"pork sausage\",\"pork sausages\",\"breakfast sausage\",\"breakfast sausages\",\"chicken sausage\",\"chicken sausages\",\"bratwurst\",\"bratwursts\"],\"decision\":\"Sausage is a seasoned grind; the ingredient list must name any curing agents beyond the meat.\",\"sources\":[{\"name\":\"Cornell LII: 9 CFR § 319.140 Sausage\",\"url\":\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/9/319.140\"},{\"name\":\"Cornell LII: 9 CFR § 319.141 Fresh pork sausage\",\"url\":\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/9/319.141\"},{\"name\":\"Cornell LII: 9 CFR § 317.17 Curing agents in the ingredients statement\",\"url\":\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/9/317.17\"}]},{\"id\":\"turkey_whole\",\"title\":\"Whole turkey\",\"aliases\":[\"whole turkey\",\"whole roasting turkey\",\"which turkey\"],\"category\":\"poultry\",\"question\":\"What should you check on a whole turkey label?\",\"decision\":\"Whole turkey without an injected basting solution.\",\"why\":\"An added basting solution is a separate formulation. Plain whole turkey is the whole-food standard.\",\"short_answer\":\"A basted or self-basted turkey has been injected or marinated with a solution. The solution and its ingredients must be identified on the label.\",\"detail\":\"The FSIS turkey guide describes solutions containing fat, broth, stock, water, spices, or other approved ingredients. Water retained during chilling is a different process from adding a flavoring solution. Fresh and frozen describe temperature history, so read the formulation on either bird.\",\"evidence_tier\":\"kristys_standard\",\"sources\":[\"https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/poultry/turkey-farm-table\",\"https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/water-meat-poultry\"],\"kristy_take\":\"An added basting solution is a separate formulation. Plain whole turkey is the whole-food standard.\",\"cart_pick\":\"Whole turkey without added solution\",\"buying_tips\":[\"Read the solution ingredients beside the product name.\",\"A frozen whole turkey can be checked for the same added-solution statement.\",\"Read retained-water wording separately from the flavoring-solution statement.\"],\"watch_out\":[\"“Natural” can appear on products containing a flavoring solution.\",\"Retained water from chilling and an injected flavoring solution are different label disclosures.\"],\"pick_steps\":[\"Beside the name, read the solution ingredients on a basted or self-basted bird.\",\"‘Natural’ can still carry a flavoring solution, so read past it.\",\"Choose fresh or frozen turkey without an injected basting solution.\"],\"science\":\"A basted or self-basted turkey has been injected or marinated with a solution. The FSIS turkey guide lists fat, broth, stock, water, spices or other approved ingredients. Water retained during chilling is a different process from adding a flavoring solution. Fresh and frozen describe temperature history, so read the formulation on either bird.\",\"labels_decoded\":[{\"term\":\"Basted or self-basted\",\"meaning\":\"Injected or marinated with a basting solution.\"},{\"term\":\"Fresh\",\"meaning\":\"A regulated temperature-history description, separate from formulation.\"}],\"tier_note\":\"Basting and added-solution statements describe documented processing; choosing a plain bird is the whole-food standard.\",\"asked_as\":[\"how do you choose a whole turkey\",\"what does self basted mean on whole turkey\",\"does this whole turkey have added brine\",\"which turkey should i buy\"]},{\"id\":\"ground_turkey\",\"title\":\"Ground turkey\",\"aliases\":[\"ground turkey\",\"minced turkey\",\"plain ground turkey\"],\"category\":\"poultry\",\"question\":\"What should you check before buying ground turkey?\",\"decision\":\"Plain ground turkey with its ingredients and fat content stated.\",\"why\":\"Grinding does not specify the fat content. A plain formulation is the whole-food standard.\",\"short_answer\":\"Ground turkey needs its own label check. US nutrition-labeling rules cover ground poultry, and a percent-lean claim must be accompanied by a percent-fat statement.\",\"detail\":\"FSIS’s archived rule covers ground turkey and requires the fat statement beside a percent-lean claim. The turkey guide says additives in processed turkey, including ground products, must be listed. The words ground turkey alone do not identify one fat level.\",\"evidence_tier\":\"kristys_standard\",\"sources\":[\"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2010-12-29/html/2010-32485.htm\",\"https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/poultry/turkey-farm-table\"],\"kristy_take\":\"Grinding does not specify the fat content. A plain formulation is the whole-food standard.\",\"cart_pick\":\"Plain ground turkey\",\"buying_tips\":[\"Choose a formulation listing turkey without added seasonings or a flavoring solution.\",\"Without a percent-lean claim, use the Nutrition Facts fat line to compare packs.\",\"Compare the fat content of the actual packs rather than assume one level for all ground turkey.\"],\"watch_out\":[\"Do not infer a fat level from the animal name. Read each pack.\"],\"pick_steps\":[\"Choose a pack listing turkey, without added seasonings or a flavoring solution.\"],\"science\":\"Grinding does not specify the fat content. FSIS’s archived rule covers ground turkey and requires a fat statement beside a percent-lean claim. The words ground turkey alone do not identify one fat level. Additives in processed turkey, including ground products, must be listed.\",\"labels_decoded\":[{\"term\":\"Percent lean\",\"meaning\":\"A composition claim that must appear with a percent-fat statement.\"}],\"tier_note\":\"The ingredient and percent-fat statements are documented label information; selecting plain ground turkey is the whole-food standard.\",\"asked_as\":[\"how do you choose ground turkey\",\"what does percent lean mean on ground turkey\",\"does ground turkey have added ingredients\"]},{\"id\":\"bacon\",\"title\":\"Bacon\",\"aliases\":[\"bacon\",\"sliced bacon\",\"packaged sliced bacon\",\"uncured bacon nitrate free\"],\"category\":\"deli\",\"question\":\"What should you check when choosing bacon?\",\"decision\":\"Bacon chosen by its curing ingredients, with the qualifier read.\",\"why\":\"The curing ingredients describe the process. An uncured claim alone does not establish nitrate-free bacon.\",\"short_answer\":\"Bacon labeled uncured can use celery powder as a natural curing source. Read the qualifier on a no-nitrates-added claim and the ingredient list.\",\"detail\":\"The archived NPR report describes natural curing sources used in bacon and hot dogs and the qualifying fine print. US curing-label policy requires curing-mixture ingredients to be listed individually. That distinguishes a source of curing ingredients, not a demonstrated health advantage.\",\"evidence_tier\":\"kristys_standard\",\"sources\":[\"https://www.npr.org/sections/thesalt/2019/08/29/755115208/duped-in-the-deli-aisle-no-nitrates-added-labels-are-often-misleading\",\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/9/317.17\"],\"kristy_take\":\"The curing ingredients describe the process. An uncured claim alone does not establish nitrate-free bacon.\",\"cart_pick\":\"Bacon with disclosed curing ingredients\",\"buying_tips\":[\"Read any exception naming naturally occurring nitrates in celery powder.\",\"Compare the disclosed curing ingredients across available bacon packs.\",\"Read each ingredient in the curing mixture, including salt, sugar, and curing agents.\"],\"watch_out\":[\"An uncured label does not by itself establish the absence of nitrates or nitrites.\",\"The source set does not establish a health advantage from choosing celery-based curing.\"],\"pick_steps\":[\"Read any exception naming nitrates naturally occurring in celery powder.\",\"Read each curing ingredient, including salt, sugar and curing agents.\",\"Compare the disclosed curing ingredients across the bacon packs on the shelf.\"],\"science\":\"Bacon labeled uncured can use celery powder as a natural curing source. An uncured label does not by itself establish the absence of nitrates or nitrites. US curing-label policy requires curing-mixture ingredients to be listed individually. The curing ingredients describe the process. The source set does not establish a health advantage from choosing celery-based curing.\",\"labels_decoded\":[{\"term\":\"Uncured\",\"meaning\":\"A label that can appear on bacon using a natural curing source; read the qualifier.\"},{\"term\":\"No nitrates added*\",\"meaning\":\"The fine print may except nitrates naturally occurring in celery powder.\"}],\"tier_note\":\"The asterisk and curing ingredients are documented label facts; choosing by them is the whole-food standard.\",\"asked_as\":[\"how do you choose bacon\",\"is uncured bacon nitrate free\",\"what does the asterisk on bacon mean\"]},{\"id\":\"hot_dogs\",\"title\":\"Hot dogs\",\"aliases\":[\"hot dog\",\"hot dogs\",\"frankfurters\",\"packaged beef hot dogs\"],\"category\":\"deli\",\"question\":\"What should you check on a pack of hot dogs?\",\"decision\":\"Hot dogs with named meat and no mechanically separated poultry.\",\"why\":\"Named meat and mechanically separated poultry are distinct ingredients. The whole-food standard favors named meat.\",\"short_answer\":\"Hot dogs can contain meat, poultry, and other declared ingredients. Mechanically separated poultry is a paste-like product, made by separating attached tissue from bones. Its name belongs in the ingredient statement.\",\"detail\":\"The US frankfurter standard distinguishes sausages with meat byproducts and requires “with byproducts” or “with variety meats” in their name. Mechanically separated poultry has its own regulatory definition. These processing distinctions support an ingredient choice, not a claim that one hot dog produces a health result.\",\"evidence_tier\":\"kristys_standard\",\"sources\":[\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/9/319.180\",\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/9/381.173\",\"https://www.npr.org/sections/thesalt/2019/08/29/755115208/duped-in-the-deli-aisle-no-nitrates-added-labels-are-often-misleading\"],\"kristy_take\":\"Named meat and mechanically separated poultry are distinct ingredients. The whole-food standard favors named meat.\",\"cart_pick\":\"Hot dogs with named meat\",\"buying_tips\":[\"Look for the named meat and read all other ingredients.\",\"Compare formulations across available packs rather than assume a species claim describes every ingredient.\",\"Check whether the list names mechanically separated chicken or turkey.\"],\"watch_out\":[\"“Uncured” can still accompany celery-based curing. Read the qualifier.\",\"The standard permits nonmeat ingredients. A beef species claim is not an ingredient list.\"],\"pick_steps\":[\"Pick named meat without mechanically separated chicken or turkey.\",\"“Uncured” can still mean celery-based curing; read the qualifier.\"],\"science\":\"The standard permits nonmeat ingredients, so a beef species claim is not an ingredient list. Compare formulations across packs rather than assume the claim describes every ingredient. Mechanically separated poultry is a paste-like product, made by separating attached tissue from bones. Sausages with meat byproducts must carry “with byproducts” or “with variety meats” in their name.\",\"labels_decoded\":[{\"term\":\"Mechanically separated poultry\",\"meaning\":\"A paste-like ingredient made by mechanically separating tissue from poultry bones.\"},{\"term\":\"With byproducts\",\"meaning\":\"Required wording for the byproduct-containing sausage formulation defined in the standard.\"}],\"tier_note\":\"The meat ingredients and byproducts wording disclose regulated processing distinctions; choosing named meat is the whole-food standard.\",\"asked_as\":[\"how do you choose hot dogs\",\"what does mechanically separated mean on hot dogs\",\"what does with byproducts mean on frankfurters\"]},{\"id\":\"chicken_breast\",\"title\":\"Chicken breast\",\"aliases\":[\"chicken breast\",\"chicken breasts\",\"plain chicken breast\"],\"category\":\"poultry\",\"question\":\"What should you check when buying chicken breast?\",\"decision\":\"Plain breast, fresh or frozen, over any with added flavoring solution.\",\"why\":\"Added flavoring solution changes the formulation. Plain chicken breast is the whole-food standard.\",\"short_answer\":\"Flavoring solutions added to poultry must be disclosed as part of the product name, including their amount. Water retained during chilling is a separate disclosure.\",\"detail\":\"FSIS describes solution added through injection, marinating, tumbling, or soaking. A product labeled natural can still contain a minimally processed flavoring solution. The archived woody-breast study also describes unusually hard breast tissue, but manual sorting was inconsistent; it does not support reliable visual identification at the case.\",\"evidence_tier\":\"kristys_standard\",\"sources\":[\"https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/water-meat-poultry\",\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7365903/\"],\"kristy_take\":\"Added flavoring solution changes the formulation. Plain chicken breast is the whole-food standard.\",\"cart_pick\":\"Plain chicken breast without added solution\",\"buying_tips\":[\"Read the full product name for the solution percentage and ingredients.\",\"Fresh or frozen breast can be checked for the same solution disclosure.\",\"Read retained-water wording separately from the added flavoring ingredients.\"],\"watch_out\":[\"“Natural” does not exclude a flavoring solution.\",\"Retained chilling water is different from an added flavoring solution.\",\"The archived woody-breast study does not establish a dependable visual test for shoppers.\"],\"pick_steps\":[\"Read the full product name for a solution percentage and its ingredients.\",\"Read past ‘natural’; it does not exclude a flavoring solution, fresh or frozen.\",\"Choose fresh or frozen plain breast without an added flavoring solution.\"],\"science\":\"FSIS describes solution added through injection, marinating, tumbling or soaking. It must be disclosed as part of the product name, including its amount. A product labeled natural can still contain a minimally processed flavoring solution. An archived study of unusually hard, woody breast tissue found manual sorting inconsistent. It does not establish a dependable visual test at the case.\",\"labels_decoded\":[{\"term\":\"Added solution\",\"meaning\":\"A disclosed flavoring formulation incorporated into the poultry.\"},{\"term\":\"Retained water\",\"meaning\":\"Water absorbed during post-slaughter processing, separately disclosed on the label.\"}],\"tier_note\":\"Any added flavoring solution has to appear in the product name, with its amount.\",\"asked_as\":[\"how do you choose chicken breast\",\"does natural chicken breast have added water\",\"what does solution added mean on chicken breast\"]},{\"id\":\"ham\",\"title\":\"Ham\",\"aliases\":[\"ham\",\"sliced ham\",\"deli ham\",\"spiral ham\",\"honey ham\",\"ham steak\",\"smoked ham\",\"country ham\",\"which ham should i buy\",\"what does water added mean on ham\"],\"category\":\"deli\",\"question\":\"Which ham should you buy?\",\"decision\":\"Ham chosen by its label class, read for added water.\",\"why\":\"Ham is the cured leg of pork. Its product name tells how much water the curing added.\",\"short_answer\":\"A ham labeled plain “Ham” must meet the highest protein level. “Water Added” and “Ham and Water Product” mean more added solution. Water appears in the ingredients either way.\",\"detail\":\"US labeling ties ham names to protein. “Ham” needs at least 20.5% protein in the lean portion. “Ham With Natural Juices” needs at least 18.5%. “Ham, Water Added” needs at least 17.0%, with 10% added solution. “Ham and Water Product” must state the percent of weight that is added ingredients. Fresh ham is the uncured leg of pork and is labeled “fresh” or “uncured”.\",\"evidence_tier\":\"kristys_standard\",\"sources\":[\"https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/meat-catfish/hams-and-food-safety\",\"https://www.canr.msu.edu/news/ham_for_the_holidays_and_all_year_long\"],\"kristy_take\":\"Ham is the cured leg of pork. Its product name tells how much water the curing added.\",\"cart_pick\":\"Ham with natural juices\",\"buying_tips\":[\"Read the product name for “Water Added” or a percent of added ingredients.\",\"Check whether the label says fully cooked or cook before eating.\",\"Find where water sits in the ingredients statement.\"],\"watch_out\":[\"“Ham and Water Product” states the share of its weight that is added ingredients.\",\"Fresh ham and country ham are not fully cooked and need cooking before eating.\"],\"pick_steps\":[\"Read the name for “Water Added” or a percent of added ingredients.\",\"Check whether the label says fully cooked or cook before eating.\"],\"science\":\"Ham is the cured leg of pork, and its product name tells how much water the curing added. Plain “Ham” must meet the highest protein level. “Ham, Water Added” needs at least 17.0% protein, with 10% added solution. “Ham and Water Product” states the share of its weight that is added ingredients. Fresh ham and country ham are not fully cooked and need cooking before eating.\",\"labels_decoded\":[{\"term\":\"Ham With Natural Juices\",\"meaning\":\"Cured ham with at least 18.5% protein.\"},{\"term\":\"Ham, Water Added\",\"meaning\":\"At least 17.0% protein, with 10% added solution.\"},{\"term\":\"Fresh ham\",\"meaning\":\"Uncured leg of pork, prepared without nitrate or nitrite.\"}],\"tier_note\":\"The water-added classes are USDA labeling definitions; choosing the least added water is the whole-food standard.\",\"asked_as\":[\"which ham should i buy\",\"what does water added mean on ham\",\"is spiral ham fully cooked\"]},{\"id\":\"turkey_bacon\",\"title\":\"Turkey bacon\",\"aliases\":[\"turkey bacon\",\"turkey bacon strips\",\"uncured turkey bacon\",\"sliced turkey bacon\"],\"category\":\"deli\",\"question\":\"What does the turkey bacon label tell you?\",\"decision\":\"Turkey bacon read by its descriptive name and curing ingredients.\",\"why\":\"Turkey bacon is turkey meat seasoned and pressed into bacon form. The label has to say which turkey meat.\",\"short_answer\":\"Bacon from turkey carries a descriptive name, such as “turkey bacon, cured turkey thigh meat”. Every curing ingredient must appear in the ingredients statement.\",\"detail\":\"Under US labeling, “bacon” alone means cured pork belly. Bacon made from poultry needs a descriptive name that says which meat was used. Each substance in a curing mixture, such as salt, sugar, or sodium nitrite, must be listed. A product made without nitrate or nitrite carries “Uncured” and “No Nitrate or Nitrite Added” beside its name.\",\"evidence_tier\":\"kristys_standard\",\"sources\":[\"https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/meat-catfish/bacon-and-food-safety\",\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/9/317.17\",\"https://health.clevelandclinic.org/turkey-bacon-how-healthy-is-it-really\"],\"kristy_take\":\"Turkey bacon is turkey meat seasoned and pressed into bacon form. The label has to say which turkey meat.\",\"cart_pick\":\"Turkey bacon with listed curing ingredients\",\"buying_tips\":[\"Read the descriptive name beside “turkey bacon” for the meat used.\",\"Read each curing ingredient in the ingredients statement.\",\"Find any “Uncured” claim and the “No Nitrate or Nitrite Added” statement with it.\"],\"watch_out\":[\"Turkey bacon is still a cured product, and its curing ingredients are on the label.\"],\"pick_steps\":[\"Read the descriptive name beside “turkey bacon” for the meat used.\",\"Read each curing ingredient in the ingredients statement.\",\"Find any “Uncured” claim and the “No Nitrate or Nitrite Added” beside it.\"],\"science\":\"Under US labeling, “bacon” alone means cured pork belly. Bacon made from poultry needs a descriptive name that says which meat was used. Turkey bacon is still a cured product. Each substance in a curing mixture, such as salt, sugar, or sodium nitrite, must be listed. A product made without added nitrate or nitrite carries “Uncured” beside its name.\",\"labels_decoded\":[{\"term\":\"Cured turkey thigh meat\",\"meaning\":\"The descriptive name that says which turkey meat the bacon is made from.\"},{\"term\":\"Uncured\",\"meaning\":\"Made without added nitrate or nitrite, stated beside the product name.\"}],\"tier_note\":\"The descriptive name and curing list are USDA labeling requirements, so reading them is the whole-food standard.\",\"asked_as\":[\"is turkey bacon cured\",\"what is turkey bacon made of\",\"how do you choose turkey bacon\"]},{\"id\":\"cured_pork\",\"title\":\"Cured pork\",\"aliases\":[\"prosciutto\",\"salami\",\"pancetta\",\"guanciale\",\"soppressata\",\"pepperoni\",\"guanciale worth buying\",\"how do you choose prosciutto\",\"how do you choose salami\",\"pork jowl\",\"pork cheek\"],\"category\":\"deli\",\"question\":\"How do you choose prosciutto, salami, pancetta, or guanciale?\",\"decision\":\"Cured pork, guanciale included, judged by its cut and curing ingredients.\",\"why\":\"Each of these is pork cured with salt and curing agents. The label names the cut and lists every curing ingredient.\",\"short_answer\":\"Prosciutto is dry-cured ham, and salami is a fermented, dried, cured ground product. Pancetta is cured belly. Guanciale is worth buying on the same terms: the cut named and the curing ingredients listed.\",\"detail\":\"Prosciutto is an Italian-style dry-cured raw ham, not smoked. Its low water content lets it be eaten raw. Salami is a fermented, dried, and cured ground meat product. Pancetta is Italian belly bacon, usually cured in salt and spices and air-dried. Cured pork from the cheeks must carry its true product name, such as pork jowl. A product made without nitrate or nitrite is labeled “Uncured” with “No Nitrate or Nitrite Added”.\",\"evidence_tier\":\"kristys_standard\",\"sources\":[\"https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/meat-catfish/hams-and-food-safety\",\"https://extension.oregonstate.edu/catalog/pnw-784-making-cured-bacon-home\",\"https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/meat-catfish/bacon-and-food-safety\",\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/9/317.17\"],\"kristy_take\":\"Each of these is pork cured with salt and curing agents. The label names the cut and lists every curing ingredient.\",\"cart_pick\":\"Cured pork with listed curing ingredients\",\"buying_tips\":[\"Read the product name for the cut, such as ham, belly, or jowl.\",\"Read each curing ingredient in the ingredients statement.\",\"Check whether the package says ready to eat or cook before eating.\"],\"watch_out\":[\"An “Uncured” product that is not dried or heavily salted must say “Not Preserved, Keep Refrigerated”.\"],\"pick_steps\":[\"Read the product name for the cut: ham, belly or jowl.\",\"Read each curing ingredient in the ingredients statement.\",\"Check whether the package says ready to eat or cook before eating.\"],\"science\":\"Each of these is pork cured with salt and curing agents. Prosciutto is dry-cured raw ham, not smoked; its low water content lets it be eaten raw. Salami is a fermented, dried and cured ground product. Pancetta is cured belly, and cheek meat must carry its true name, such as pork jowl. An “Uncured” product that is not dried or 